WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.684 --> 00:00:02.770
(dramatic music)

00:00:02.770 --> 00:00:04.160
- In December of last year,

00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:07.930
doctors in Wuhan, China had
a mystery on their hands.

00:00:07.930 --> 00:00:09.430
People in the region were coming down

00:00:09.430 --> 00:00:11.520
with an unusual pneumonia.

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:12.525
It's cold and flu season,

00:00:12.525 --> 00:00:15.050
but these people didn't have either.

00:00:15.050 --> 00:00:18.310
Doctors quickly realized it
might be something entirely new.

00:00:18.310 --> 00:00:20.810
- Medical nightmare, the
coronavirus outbreak.

00:00:20.810 --> 00:00:22.240
- Never seen before coronavirus.

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:25.720
- The number of confirmed
cases in China keeps soaring.

00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:27.260
- [Newscaster] Already 45 cases.

00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:28.248
- [Broadcaster] 200 Cases.

00:00:28.248 --> 00:00:29.081
- [Female Broadcaster] There more than

00:00:29.081 --> 00:00:30.387
6,000 confirmed cases.

00:00:30.387 --> 00:00:33.075
- But so far, this outbreak
has gone differently

00:00:33.075 --> 00:00:35.320
from others in the past.

00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:37.277
Thanks to the latest in biotechnology,

00:00:37.277 --> 00:00:39.860
scientists have been able
to unravel the mystery

00:00:39.860 --> 00:00:41.220
at remarkable speed.

00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:43.260
They're revealing the
biology of the new virus

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:44.900
more quickly than ever before.

00:00:44.900 --> 00:00:46.620
And they're hoping it's fast enough

00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:48.683
to help drive back the disease outbreak.

00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:56.170
Chinese officials told the
World Health Organization

00:00:56.170 --> 00:00:58.350
that it had an outbreak
of some kind of virus

00:00:58.350 --> 00:01:00.120
on December 31st.

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:02.900
By January 7th, Chinese
scientists figured out

00:01:02.900 --> 00:01:05.160
that the virus was a Coronavirus.

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:08.140
That's from the same
family of viruses as SARS

00:01:08.140 --> 00:01:09.896
which emerged in China in 2002

00:01:09.896 --> 00:01:13.773
and MERS which infected people
in the Middle East in 2012.

00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:16.780
The next step in
understanding the new virus

00:01:16.780 --> 00:01:18.390
was sequencing its genome.

00:01:18.390 --> 00:01:20.359
Figuring out the unique
order of nucleotides,

00:01:20.359 --> 00:01:23.171
genetic building blocks that make it up.

00:01:23.171 --> 00:01:26.740
The new coronavirus
genome looks like this.

00:01:26.740 --> 00:01:29.210
It's just under 30,000 bases long.

00:01:29.210 --> 00:01:31.070
The coronavirus is made of a type

00:01:31.070 --> 00:01:33.600
of genetic material called RNA.

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:35.040
But, scientists convert it

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:37.040
to the more recognizable DNA

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:38.820
to make it easier to study.

00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:40.470
That's what you're seeing here.

00:01:40.470 --> 00:01:43.703
By January, 13, less than a
week after it was identified,

00:01:43.703 --> 00:01:45.900
scientists in Shanghai had knocked out

00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:48.410
the entire genetic
sequence of a virus sample

00:01:48.410 --> 00:01:50.490
collected from a patient.

00:01:50.490 --> 00:01:52.260
Compared though with the SARS virus,

00:01:52.260 --> 00:01:55.160
which started infecting
people in November 2002;

00:01:55.160 --> 00:01:57.465
it took until April 2003 before that virus

00:01:57.465 --> 00:02:00.130
was sequenced in its entirety.

00:02:00.130 --> 00:02:01.070
And at this point,

00:02:01.070 --> 00:02:02.970
we don't just have one genetic sequence

00:02:02.970 --> 00:02:04.610
for the new coronavirus.

00:02:04.610 --> 00:02:06.830
Scientists have collected
and sequenced nearly

00:02:06.830 --> 00:02:08.950
two dozen samples of the virus,

00:02:08.950 --> 00:02:10.780
collected from different patients.

00:02:10.780 --> 00:02:14.210
Researchers say the speed
has been unprecedented.

00:02:14.210 --> 00:02:16.300
A lot of important things become possible

00:02:16.300 --> 00:02:19.340
once experts have this
full sequence of the virus.

00:02:19.340 --> 00:02:22.300
Notably, that blueprint can
end up in a place like this.

00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:25.610
- Yeah, this is the exact typical,

00:02:25.610 --> 00:02:28.690
all that we have is a
bunch of A, C, G and T.

00:02:28.690 --> 00:02:31.470
And for us and our machine
we can print anything.

00:02:31.470 --> 00:02:34.360
So if you want hemoglobin
today, we can print hemoglobin.

00:02:34.360 --> 00:02:36.060
If you want chicken DNA,

00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:40.020
if you want coronavirus DNA, we can do it.

00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:42.240
- Twist Bioscience is a biotech company

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:45.810
in south San Francisco that
builds custom DNA to order.

00:02:45.810 --> 00:02:47.527
As the coronavirus outbreak progresses,

00:02:47.527 --> 00:02:50.360
scientists around the world
are turning to companies

00:02:50.360 --> 00:02:53.750
like Twist to build copies of
viral genes for urgent study.

00:02:53.750 --> 00:02:56.000
And they're sending in lots of orders.

00:02:56.000 --> 00:02:57.920
- We expected that there would be

00:02:59.350 --> 00:03:03.873
a number of demands for
variants of the coronavirus

00:03:03.873 --> 00:03:07.914
to help in that discovery of drugs,

00:03:07.914 --> 00:03:10.560
vaccines, and diagnostic kits.

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:13.419
And, whatever comes from the web tonight,

00:03:13.419 --> 00:03:16.450
we'll start making tomorrow.

00:03:16.450 --> 00:03:19.600
- Custom built DNA has been
around since the 1980s,

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:21.840
but the technology has
gotten dramatically cheaper

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:23.760
and easier since then.

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:25.800
So researchers today have a lot more DNA

00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:27.330
at their disposal than they did during

00:03:27.330 --> 00:03:29.834
the SARs or MERS outbreaks.

00:03:29.834 --> 00:03:30.841
- [Emily] If you go back to 2000,

00:03:30.841 --> 00:03:34.640
it would take $10.00 to
make one base of DNA.

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:38.400
In 2010, it will make
$1.00, so ten times more.

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:41.100
And today, it's $00.09 per base,

00:03:41.100 --> 00:03:43.350
so again, ten times more.

00:03:43.350 --> 00:03:45.370
- That's important, because
this kind of research

00:03:45.370 --> 00:03:47.620
requires a lot of trial and error.

00:03:47.620 --> 00:03:50.920
So the more copies of a gene
scientists have, the better.

00:03:50.920 --> 00:03:52.170
- So it's biology.

00:03:52.170 --> 00:03:56.390
And in biology it almost
never works on the first time.

00:03:56.390 --> 00:03:59.410
You have to take a lot of
shots on goals to get there.

00:03:59.410 --> 00:04:00.710
We give people the opportunity

00:04:00.710 --> 00:04:03.557
to take more shots on goals faster.

00:04:03.557 --> 00:04:08.250
And that way they can get
to that answer faster.

00:04:08.250 --> 00:04:09.620
- There's a few things scientists

00:04:09.620 --> 00:04:11.600
do with this genetic material.

00:04:11.600 --> 00:04:12.670
It can help create tests

00:04:12.670 --> 00:04:14.610
to screen patients for the virus.

00:04:14.610 --> 00:04:15.900
It can help researchers understand

00:04:15.900 --> 00:04:18.730
how the virus works and what
it does to cells in the body.

00:04:18.730 --> 00:04:20.230
And crucially, it could be used

00:04:20.230 --> 00:04:22.300
in the development of vaccines.

00:04:22.300 --> 00:04:23.670
One important note,

00:04:23.670 --> 00:04:25.750
even though companies like
Twist are churning out

00:04:25.750 --> 00:04:27.870
copies of the coronavirus genome,

00:04:27.870 --> 00:04:30.860
there's no concern they'll
accidentally start an epidemic.

00:04:30.860 --> 00:04:32.910
They're not actually making the virus.

00:04:32.910 --> 00:04:35.340
- I think one thing that
people should remember

00:04:35.340 --> 00:04:39.676
is that nature is the
biggest bio-terrorist.

00:04:39.676 --> 00:04:41.743
You know, nature wants to kill you,

00:04:41.743 --> 00:04:44.240
and that's what happens
with the coronavirus.

00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:46.150
So, the virus is out there.

00:04:46.150 --> 00:04:47.750
The sequence is out there.

00:04:47.750 --> 00:04:50.063
So, we are not creating more virus.

00:04:50.063 --> 00:04:52.615
We are making fragments
of the virus to research

00:04:52.615 --> 00:04:57.615
in order to enable them
to find tools to detect it

00:04:57.631 --> 00:04:59.843
and tools to fight it.

00:05:03.064 --> 00:05:03.897
(dramatic music)

00:05:03.897 --> 00:05:06.230
- The coronavirus is
still a fast developing

00:05:06.230 --> 00:05:07.600
public health crisis.

00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:09.230
It'll be awhile before we fully

00:05:09.230 --> 00:05:10.810
understand its health impact.

00:05:10.810 --> 00:05:12.760
And longer still, before
there are treatments

00:05:12.760 --> 00:05:14.920
or vaccines available to fight it.

00:05:14.920 --> 00:05:16.633
But, thanks to the progress in DNA tech,

00:05:16.640 --> 00:05:18.980
we have a greater head
start than ever before.

00:05:20.900 --> 00:05:21.600
Thanks so much for watching.

00:05:22.160 --> 00:05:27.280
If you're interested in learning more about the coronavirus, check out theverge.com for the latest news on the ongoing outbreak.

